          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Spanish Civil War Impact</strong><br>\nOrwell's participation in the Spanish Civil War in 1936 was a pivotal moment in his life. He joined the Republican forces fighting against Franco's Fascists and was shot in the throat, an injury that affected his voice. The war's betrayals and political machinations deepened his disdain for totalitarianism, which heavily influenced his later work, particularly \"Homage to Catalonia.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Orwell's Political Views</strong><br>\nA profound political consciousness characterizes Orwell's work. He was a democratic socialist who advocated for social justice and opposed totalitarianism in any form. His experiences in Spain, witnessing the distortion of truth by political propaganda, cemented his determination to defend truth and transparency in politics through his writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Animal Farm\" and Allegory</strong><br>\nAnimal Farm, published in 1945, is one of Orwell's most famous works. This allegorical novella reflects events leading up to the Russian Revolution of 1917 and then on to the Stalinist era of the Soviet Union. Orwell uses a farm's anthropomorphized animals to illustrate the corrupting influence of power and the perversion of revolutionary ideals.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
