          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"1984\" and Its Legacy</strong><br>\nOrwell's last novel, \"1984,\" published in 1949, is a dystopian masterpiece about a future totalitarian state where the government controls thought and reality. The novel introduced terms like \"Big Brother,\" \"doublethink,\" and \"thoughtcrime,\" which have since entered the common lexicon as symbols of oppressive governance. It remains a critical work for understanding the dangers of authoritarianism.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Journalistic Contributions</strong><br>\nBesides novels, Orwell was a prolific journalist and essayist. His essays cover a wide range of topics, from personal reflections to political commentary, and are noted for their clarity, insight, and directive style. \"Shooting an Elephant\" and \"Politics and the English Language\" are among his most acclaimed essays, demonstrating his skill in prose and argumentation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Orwell's Death and Influence</strong><br>\nOrwell died of tuberculosis in 1950, but his work continues to influence political thought and popular culture. His writing is celebrated for its prophetic qualities and its ability to dissect the complex social and political issues of his time, which remain relevant today. His legacy as a critic of totalitarianism and advocate for human rights endures through his novels, essays, and journalistic works.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
